{"name":"Tetsu Iron","hex":"#455765","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"iron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, inexpensive metal, silvery grey when untarnished, that rusts, is attracted by magnets, and is used in making steel: a chemical element having atomic number 26 and symbol Fe.","Any material, not a steel, predominantly made of elemental iron."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the metal iron.","Strong , inflexible."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tetsu","blocks":[{"text":"Tetsu is a masculine Japanese given name. Notable people with the name include:"},{"items":["Tetsu Iida , Japanese photographer","Tetsu Inada , Japanese voice actor","Tetsu Inoue (テツ・イノウエ), Japanese musician","Tetsu Katayama , Japanese politician and Prime Minister of Japan","Tetsu Komai , Japanese-American actor","Tetsu Nagasawa , Japanese footballer and manager","Tetsu Nakamura, Japanese physician","Tetsu Nakamura (actor), Japanese film actor and opera singer","Tetsu Nishikawa , Japanese golfer","Tetsu Sawaki , Japanese actor","Tetsu Shiratori , Japanese voice actor","Tetsu Sugiyama , Japanese footballer","Tetsu Takano , Japanese musician","Tetsu Watanabe , Japanese actor","Tetsu Yamauchi , real name Tetsuo Yamauchi, Japanese musician","Tetsu Yano , Japanese science fiction translator and writer","Tetsushi Mizokami, Japanese founder of Uncle Tetsu's Cheesecake","Tetsuya (musician), formerly known as Tetsu, a musician"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetsu","wikidata":"Q3519203"},{"title":"Iron","blocks":[{"text":"Iron is a chemical element; it has symbol Fe and atomic number 26. It is a metal that belongs to the first transition series and group 8 of the periodic table. It is, by mass, the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most abundant element in the Earth's crust. In its metallic state it was mainly deposited by meteorites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron","wikidata":"Q677"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Ironattenuation.PNG/500px-Ironattenuation.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Ironattenuation.PNG/1280px-Ironattenuation.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Ironattenuation.PNG/500px-Ironattenuation.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Photon mass attenuation coefficient for iron","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIronattenuation.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Widmanstatten_hand.jpg/500px-Widmanstatten_hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Widmanstatten_hand.jpg/1280px-Widmanstatten_hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Widmanstatten_hand.jpg/500px-Widmanstatten_hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" A polished and chemically etched piece of an iron meteorite, believed to be similar in composition to the Earth's metallic core, showing individual crystals of the iron-nickel alloy (Widmanstatten pattern)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWidmanstatten_hand.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Roussillon_sentier_des_ocres2.JPG/500px-Roussillon_sentier_des_ocres2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars